This is a problem with fractions.  Notice that 3, 6, 9, ... are all multiples of 3, so if we want to find the probability that a random number chosen from 1-100 is a multiple of 3, it is only necessary to find how many numbers there are that are multiples of 3.  The first multiple of 3 is 3, and the last is 99.  The only trick here is counting every multiple of 3 in that range.

We start at 3, and each number that follows adds 3 to the previous number.  We are adding 3 to the previous number as we go along.  Notice the pattern.  At the end of the sequence we have 3, 6, 9, ..., 99.  There are 30 numbers we add 3 to to reach 99.  For each of those numbers, we add 3 to the previous number, so we have counted that many numbers for every multiple of 3.  30/30 = 1/1 = 1.  The answer is 1/1 = \boxed{1}.ilibususer
